NAME
       winrm-shell - a PowerShell session on a Windows host (Cyber Range)

SYNOPSIS
       winrm-shell -i HOST -u USER [-p PASSWORD | -H HASH]

DESCRIPTION
       winrm-shell signs in to the remote management service of a Windows
       computer (WinRM, TCP port 5985) and gives you a PowerShell prompt there,
       marked with a * at the start:

           *PS C:\Users\kim\Documents>

       Everything you type runs on the Windows host as that account until you
       type exit. A standard account can't open other people's profile folders
       or protected folders, and may write only to its own profile, Public and
       the temp folders; an administrator may do anything.

       Only accounts that may use remote management (members of Remote
       Management Users, or administrators) can sign in.

       In Cyber Range the Windows hosts are pretend machines with a simulated
       PowerShell 5.1 (the same one as Cert Lab's PowerShell course).

OPTIONS
       -i HOST     the Windows host's address or name
       -u USER     the account (DOMAIN\USER for a domain account)
       -p PASSWORD its password (without -p or -H winrm-shell asks)
       -H HASH     sign in with the account's password hash instead of the
                   password. Windows accepts a hash for network sign-ins, so a
                   stolen hash is as good as the password ("pass the hash").
                   The range's hashes are its own simulated format.

EXTRA COMMANDS
       download FILE [LOCAL]   copy a file from the Windows host to your box
       upload LOCAL [FILE]     copy a file from your box to the Windows host
       exit                    leave

EXAMPLES
       winrm-shell -i 10.10.1.30 -u kim -p pancake
       whoami
       Get-ChildItem C:\Users\kim\Desktop
       exit

NOTES
       Only test computers you own or have written permission to test.

SEE ALSO
       smbclient(1), hashcrack(1), ticket-request(1)
